--TEST--
`Core\Http\Stream::events` reads the WHATWG EventSource format whichever of `\r\n`, `\r` and `\n` ended each line, keeps an `id` in force between events, dispatches nothing for a block carrying no `data`, and is the one framing a lone `\r` ends a line for — `rule:http-server/a-streamed-reply-is-bounded-by-idle-and-a-lifetime`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Http\Event::data
// covers: Core\Http\Event::name
// covers: Core\Http\Event::id
// Nothing below opens a socket or resolves a name: every outbound call is
// answered from the table (`rule:testing/an-outbound-call-is-answered-from-a-table`),
// which is what lets a streamed reply be written out here byte for byte.
//
// One reply, three times, differing only in what ends each line. A parser that
// learned two of the three terminators reads the third as one unterminated line
// and dispatches nothing at all, so the three are asked the same question and
// the answers are compared rather than each being read on its own.
string $lf = ": a comment, which is skipped\nretry: 500\nevent: tick\ndata: one\nid: 7\n\ndata: two\ndata: and more\n\n";
string $crlf = ": a comment, which is skipped\r\nretry: 500\r\nevent: tick\r\ndata: one\r\nid: 7\r\n\r\ndata: two\r\ndata: and more\r\n\r\n";
string $cr = ": a comment, which is skipped\rretry: 500\revent: tick\rdata: one\rid: 7\r\rdata: two\rdata: and more\r\r";

Core\Test::answerHttp("https://sse.example.com/lf", 200, {body: $lf, headers: ["content-type" => "text/event-stream"]});
Core\Test::answerHttp("https://sse.example.com/crlf", 200, {body: $crlf, headers: ["content-type" => "text/event-stream"]});
Core\Test::answerHttp("https://sse.example.com/cr", 200, {body: $cr, headers: ["content-type" => "text/event-stream"]});

array<string> $urls = ["https://sse.example.com/lf", "https://sse.example.com/crlf", "https://sse.example.com/cr"];
foreach ($urls as string $url) {
    // The head is readable before the body is read at all, and it is the same
    // reading `Core\Http\Response` gives — one implementation behind two doors.
    var $stream = Core\Http\Client::stream(Core\Http\Method::Get, $url);
    echo $stream->status(), " ", $stream->header("content-type") ?? "no type", " ", Core\Arr::count($stream->headers("content-type")), "\n";

    // Two events, not three: the leading block sets `retry` and a comment and
    // carries no `data`, so it dispatches nothing — while the `id` it would
    // have carried stays in force for the block after the one that set it,
    // which is why the second event reports `7` without naming it.
    foreach ($stream->events() as Core\Http\Event $event) {
        echo $event->name() ?? "message", " id=", $event->id() ?? "none", " [", $event->data(), "]\n";
    }
}

// The one difference between the two framings, named on the side that does not
// have it: `lines()` ends a line at `\n` alone, so the body every line of which
// ended at `\r` is one line to it and six lines plus two events to `events()`.
var $flat = Core\Http\Client::stream(Core\Http\Method::Get, "https://sse.example.com/cr");
int $lines = 0;
foreach ($flat->lines() as tainted string $line) {
    $lines = $lines + 1;
}
echo "lines: ", $lines, "\n";
--EXPECT--
200 text/event-stream 1
tick id=7 [one]
message id=7 [two
and more]
200 text/event-stream 1
tick id=7 [one]
message id=7 [two
and more]
200 text/event-stream 1
tick id=7 [one]
message id=7 [two
and more]
lines: 1
